being clicked dominant refers to a  tendency to rely on the gluteus maximus  that but more than quadriceps for  squatting type movements many people  believe that this is one of the key  determining factors predicting athletic  performance and while the term itself is  a little overplayed and may paint an  oversimplified picture is certainly true  that many of us struggled to properly  activate ugly's during movements that  should feature them front and center if  like many people you are currently quad  dominant then making a few changes to  your training may help you to tap into  greater explosive strength this makes a  ton of sense when you consider that the  gluteus maximus is the largest muscle in  the human body and that its main role as  propulsion jumping climbing and  sprinting it's often referred to as the  antigravity muscle and of course if  you're trying to build big glutes  because you think it looks good then you  need to activate the glutes properly  otherwise you won't get any benefits  from all of those squats if you perform  a large number of squats and you notice  that your quads are more fatigued at the  end and your glutes there was a large  chance that they are playing a  disproportionately big role in that  movement thus you are quad dominant you  might also notice that your lower back  does more work during back squats and  you may encounter knee pain quad  dominance can be somewhat genetic but  for many of us there was a simple fix  use flatter shoes without a heel to toe  drop and work more on your mobility  here's the thing if you're performing  squats using high structured shoes that  raise your heels off the ground then  that's going to force more weight onto  the balls of your feet especially as you  drop into the bottom of the squat you  are centrally leaning forwards this also  means that you now require much less  ankle mobility to get to the bottom see  this for yourself try performing a  pistol squat with shoes on and make a  note of how easy it is and whether you  fall over now perform the same thing  barefooted you'll find that it's easier  to stay balanced with the shoe on why  because you're being slightly tipped  forwards thus ensuring you don't fall  backwards with the shoe off however you  now need more ankle mobility to lean  forwards and to avoid falling the weight  is further back in relation to your  heels pistol squats with him without  heeled shoes are two entirely different  movements remove your shoes or use  barefoot shoes during the Scotland and  you'll be able to engage your glutes  more to further enhance this effect  you'll need to be more mindful of the  way you're moving during the squat that  means you want to focus on driving  through the  the foot the other problem of course is  that many of us spend all day sitting on  our buttocks this not only weakens the  muscles but it also deadens our  proprioceptive feedback entirely  eventually we actually lose some of the  ability to communicate with the gluts  thereby making it much harder to  activate them during training this is  then combined with unwanted adaptations  and lost mobility such as tighter hip  flexors which makes it extremely  difficult to drive through the posterior  chain a very simple way to solve this  one is to take regular breaks throughout  the day and again to a low resting squat  I do this in the morning as part of a  morning routine and anytime I get up to  make coffee it's made a huge difference  to my mobility and the flatus ISM and  just as useful is to include a few slow  air squats before starting my workout  proper and taking the time to feel which  muscles are activating but of course  when jumping during sports or in parkour  you don't actually drop into a deep  squat first more often you push off the  ball of your foot with only a slight  bend at the knee this is something that  JC Santana pointed out to me during a  recent chat so the answer here is to  make sure you're using your leg properly  by landing on the ball of the foot when  running or leading up to a jump this  while keeping the knee and ankle  directly below the center of gravity is  what will allow you to compress your  joints properly to absorb the impact and  return the energy through the ground  rather than just jolting through your  knees this is also what allows to  properly engage the glutes and it's also  another big advantage of going barefoot  or using minimal shoes author chung she  speaks at length about the hyper arch  mechanism he explains how forming the  stronger arch in the foots by landing on  the balls of the feet not the toes can  help to activate the glutes even more  then during propulsion starting with a  slight dorsiflexion of the toes this  will allow you to drive through the big  toe and activate the glutes even more in  short your leg acts like a kind of  spring allowing your hamstrings glutes  and quads to work together as they're  supposed to and your big toe is able to  help drive even more force through the  ground the big toe and the glutes are  necessarily connected seeing as the  glutes aid in external rotation of the  hip and this also makes a ton of sense  when you consider how many movements  from jumping movements to talking  movements involve driving power through  the big toe the toes are the bridge  between the massive muscles in our lower  body and the ground this is also why  according to Thomas Meyers we have  fascial connections called trains that  run from our feet to many other  muscles in our upper bodies because we  use them all together so often again  this is something you'll lose if you  constantly wear thick shoes that prevent  you from engaging your toes and  encourage you to land on your heel when  running the hallucis longus which is the  toe flex are responsible for the big toe  has also been shown to respond to direct  training providing improvement in  running speed at jumping height balance  and more my favorite options for toe  training are to use an isometric hold  trying to push the big toe into the  ground a trick used by ballet dancers  another option is to use calf jumps or  calf raises while being mindful to  really engage the big toes likewise we  can improve glute strength and  activation with specific targeted  training for the glutes glute bridges in  particular help to not only strengthen  the gliese but also stretch the hip  flexors and counteract the anterior  pelvic tilt they'll stabilize the knees  and help to be more mindful when running  and squatting another great one is step  ups do these with a barbell and you'll  be forced to drive through your  posterior chain with no option to let  the quads take over this is a fantastic  movement in general actually as it also  requires you to stabilize the hips and  to push off of a single leg two things  which again reflect the way we actually  use our legs in the real world another  thing you can try is a box squat and  here you're placing a box or something  else behind you as you squat down and  that allows you to really get down into  the squat without worrying about falling  over backwards and then to drive  yourself up through your buttocks  notice that these recommendations are  compounded not isolation isolating the  hamstrings and quads with resistance  machines and building them up  disproportionately might have actually  contributed to this issue for many of us  in the first place the aim is to  practice using these muscles together as  they were designed to be used and of  course we don't want to become glute  dominant at the expense of proper  balance either which is again why I  think a more appropriate term is glute  activation hope you found this video  useful interesting guys if you did then  please leave a like and share around  that helps me out immensely subscribe if  you want more like this let me know in  the comments down below how you train  your glutes and whether glute activation  has been a problem for you in the past  if you like this kind of training for  performance first then you'll probably  enjoy my ebook and training program  super functional training you can find a  link to that in the description down  below and there's a discount on right  now whilst many people are still in  lockdown thanks a ton for watching this  one and I'll see you soon bye for now
 

